        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`clamp` now raises `ValueError` when `low > high`, and your standard test command will fail if anyone brings the bug back.\n\n**The fix (`bounds.py`):** `clamp` checks the bounds before doing anything else. If `low` is greater than `high`, it raises `ValueError` with a message that shows both values. Valid bounds give the same results as before, including when `low == high`.\n\n**The guardrail (`test_bounds.py`):** I added two tests, which `python3 -m unittest discover -v` picks up automatically:\n- `test_low_greater_than_high_raises` checks for `ValueError` with reversed bounds `(10, 0)`, using values below, inside, at the edges of and above that range.\n- `test_equal_bounds` checks that `low == high` still works, so a future fix can't wrongly use `>=`.\n\n**Checked:**\n- All 3 tests pass with the fix.\n- I put the old `bounds.py` back temporarily and ran the tests again. The new test failed for all 5 values, so it does catch the bug if it's reintroduced. Then I restored the fix."
        }
      ],
